From romain.francois at dbmail.com  Sat Aug  1 09:41:56 2009
From: romain.francois at dbmail.com (Romain Francois)
Date: Sat, 01 Aug 2009 11:41:56 +0200
Subject: issues with NA when serializing REXPDouble
Message-ID: <4A740DE4.9030401@dbmail.com>

Hello,

I'm trying to serialize REXP objects. With a simple modification to the 
REXP class (adding implements java.io.Serializable) it mostly works, but 
I have some trouble with NA values used in the REXPDouble class.

Basically, REXPDouble uses a special NaN double value to indicate NA :

public static final double NA = 
Double.longBitsToDouble(0x7ff00000000007a2L);

but java serialization does not care about it and java completely 
disregards the bit state when it writes the object, apparently the 
writeDouble method relies on the |Double.doubleToLongBits method instead 
of the ||Double.doubleToRawLongBits|. I've put here the 
SerializationTest class that show the loss of data: 
http://addictedtor.free.fr/misc/rosuda/SerializationTest.java

$ runjri SerializationTest
before serialization : 2.0 : 4611686018427387904 : false
after serialization  : 2.0 : 4611686018427387904 : false

before serialization : NaN : 9221120237041092514 : true
after serialization  : NaN : 9221120237041090560 : false

My idea to circumvent this at the moment is to add a field to the class 
that identifies the index of the NA values, something like :

private int[] na_index = null ;

and then use it only in private methods readObject and writeObject. I've 
implemented this ( 
http://addictedtor.free.fr/misc/rosuda/REXPDouble-serialization.diff ), 
and it seems to do the trick:

$ java SerializationTest
before serialization : 2.0 : 4611686018427387904 : false
after serialization  : 2.0 : 4611686018427387904 : false

before serialization : NaN : 9221120237041092514 : true
after serialization  : NaN : 9221120237041092514 : true

Romain

