reduce the problem to the basics.

import junit.framework.TestCase;
import org.rosuda.JRI.Rengine;

public class JRITest extends TestCase {   
    private Rengine engine = null;

    protected void setUp() throws Exception {
        engine = new Rengine(new String[]{"--vanilla"},false,null);
    }
   
    protected void tearDown() throws Exception {
        if (engine != null)
            engine.end();
    }
   
    public void testAssign() {
        int iterations = 100;
        int numRows = 10000;
        int numColumns = 10000;
       
        double[] column = new double[numRows];
        long[] columnRefs = new long[numColumns];

        for (int i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
            System.out.println("putting 
matrix["+numRows+","+numColumns+"]: "+i);
            for (int c = 0; c < numColumns; c++)
                columnRefs[c] = engine.rniPutDoubleArray(column);
            long dataFrameRef = engine.rniPutVector(columnRefs);
           
            System.out.println("assigning 
matrix["+numRows+","+numColumns+"]: "+i);
            engine.rniAssign("matrix",dataFrameRef,0);
            engine.rniEval(engine.rniParse("rm(list=ls())",1),0);
        }
    }

    public void testRetrieve() {
        int iterations = 100;
        int numRows = 1000;
        int numColumns = 700;
       
        double[] column = new double[numRows];
        long[] columnRefs = new long[numColumns];

        for (int c = 0; c < numColumns; c++)
            columnRefs[c] = engine.rniPutDoubleArray(column);
        long dataFrameRef = engine.rniPutVector(columnRefs);
        engine.rniAssign("matrix",dataFrameRef,0);
       
        for (int i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
            System.out.println("evaluating 
matrix["+numRows+","+numColumns+"]: "+i);
            for (int c = 0; c < numColumns; c++)
                columnRefs[c] = 
engine.rniEval(engine.rniParse("matrix["+(c+1)+"]",1),0);
           
//            System.out.println("getting 
matrix["+numRows+","+numColumns+"]: "+i);
//            for (int c = 0; c < numColumns; c++)
//                engine.rniGetDoubleArray(columnRefs[c]);
        }
    }
}

The testAssign() method never causes any exception, even with 100 
iterations on a matrix of 10000x10000 doubles.
The testRetriece() method however causes the EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION 
when either the size of the matrix is too large, or the number of 
iterations it to high. In my configuration f.e., it fails on a matrix of 
1000x1000 after 13 iterations. Reducing the size of the matrix causes it 
to fail after more iterations. Since failure occurred during the 
rniEval() call, I commented the actual retrieval of the double arrays, 
with the same results.
So the suspicion is that some memory is being consumed an never freed 
during the rniEval() call, related to the size of the matrix that is 
being retrieved.

Any suggestions ?

From simon.urbanek at r-project.org  Thu Aug 13 13:39:03 2009
From: simon.urbanek at r-project.org (Simon Urbanek)
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2009 09:39:03 -0400
Subject: Re: EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION when calling rniEval() to retrieve arrays
In-Reply-To: <4A83F760.50506@isencia.com>
