Message-ID: <CFCF5F4C-9AD3-4094-AE92-0D4148F7B3F8@r-project.org>

Jan,

the problem is in your code - you're using RNI instead of the high- 
level API, so you're responsible for memory protection - which you  
don't do. If you really want to use the low-level API, you should read  
Writing R Extensions with care (see PROTECT/UNPROTECT). Once the  
references are protected properly, the example works without problems:

             for (int c = 0; c < numColumns; c++)
                 engine.rniProtect(columnRefs[c] =  
engine.rniPutDoubleArray(column));
             long dataFrameRef = engine.rniPutVector(columnRefs);
             engine.rniAssign("matrix",dataFrameRef,0);
             engine.rniUnprotect(numColumns);

(If you want to use the results of rniEval, you have to protect them  
as well).

Also note that your rni calls are not synchronized via a mutex, so you  
must make sure there is no other thread with access to the engine.

Again, all this complexity is hidden if you use the high-level API  
(parseAndEval(), assign(), ...) so I would suggest using that unless  
you really want to get dirty ;).

Cheers,
Simon


