>> I can't recall the specific crashes. We have not used the code in over a 
>> year. JRI seems to be getting  progressively more robust. You've created a 
>> really useful tool that just  seems to be getting better by the day. I was 
>> mainly referring to the JNI mechanism in  general, we've noticed in other 
>> JNI based packages that when something  goes awry the JVM tends to crash. 
>> Although as I mentioned in my earlier  thread about windows for some reason 
>> calling the default REngine constructor seems to crash it,
>> 
>> >
>>> 
>>>> We're considering using Rserve to isolate the R functionality from the 
>>>> main application JVM. I see a note in the Rserve documentation 
>>>> recommending against using Rserve on windows. Can anybody elaborate on the 
>>>> deficiencies and how severe they are?
>>> 
>>> The main difference is the lack of fork() in Windows so parallel 
>>> connections are not supported. Quote from Rserve for Window release notes 
>>> (consider reading the full document):
>>> http://rforge.net/Rserve/rserve-win.html
>>> 
>>> Please read before downloading/using the Windows version of Rserve!
>>> The Windows version of Rserve is slightly limited compared to its unix 
>>> counterpart. Quick overview of the differences: (for technical details see 
>>> bottom of this page)
>>>
>>> 	• the config file is Rserv.cfg in the working directory (unless 
>>> changed at compile-time)
>>> 	• initialization of R_HOME is performed automatically by fetching the 
>>> information from the registry, i.e. you should use R installed by the 
>>> official Windows installer and leave the DCOM option checked (this is the 
>>> default) unless you know what you're doing. You should copy Rserve.exe in 
>>> the bin directory of such installed R.
>>> 	• no parallel connections are supported, subsequent connections share 
>>> the same namespace
>>> 	• sessions are not supported - this is a consequence of the fact that 
>>> parallel connections are not supported
>>> 	• no error handling - always use "try(...)" when evaluating commands, 
>>> otherwise the server may die on an error
>>> 	• local unix sockets are not supported (by design, as the name 
>>> implies)
>>> 
>>> 
>>>> Is Rserve stable or would you recommend we use JRI or something else 
>>>> entirely?
>>>> 
>>> 
>>> Rserve is stable (it's in fact older than the JRI project), however on 
>>> Windows you have to make sure you're trapping errors (see above).
