From o.mannion at auckland.ac.nz  Mon Aug 31 04:04:33 2009
From: o.mannion at auckland.ac.nz
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2009 16:04:33 +1200
Subject: JRIEngine vs Rengine & usage of org.rosuda.REngine
Message-ID: <6085BF643DA19E43B24174695D9A63F51D944F579E@artsmail4.ARTSNET.AUCKLAND.AC.NZ>

Hi,

I'm trying to understand the difference between

org.rosuda.REngine.REngine
* an abstraction layer that can support different implementations of an R connection (eg: via JRI, or via RServe)
* additional "middle-ware" type functions between a low level (JRI/RServe) and an application (eg: JGR).

and

org.rosuda.JRI.Rengine
* a subclass of Thread which calls native methods in jri.dll

(It's unfortunate that there is a Rengine and REngine class - this makes it quite confusing!)

org.rosuda.REngine.JRI.JRIEngine is an implementation of org.rosuda.REngine.REngine which:
* is used by JGR
* encapsulates an instance of org.rosuda.JRI.Rengine

The "middle-ware" functionalities of org.rosuda.REngine.REngine appear to be:
* ensure single thread execution
* a class hierarchy of java objects (REXP, RList, REXPVector etc.) which represent R objects and a set of methods to convert between the two.

I'm wondering:
* Is org.rosuda.REngine a public API that I can use in my application? Is it stable?
* Is there any documentation, other than the javadocs, which explains the features and usage of org.rosuda.REngine? 

Thanks for your help!


